</ul><h1>Seasoning's Greetings</h1><p>I didn’t really get into cooking until after college. It’s not that I <em>never</em> cooked when I was younger, but the stuff I made in those early days was definitely rudimentary. And even now, I can’t claim to be some pioneer in the kitchen. I’m mostly following recipes, maybe making a tweak here or there, or combining two, or what have you.</p>
<p>It’s fun, I think, watching as a bunch of seemingly disparate ingredients come together to make something greater than the sum of their parts. And often, it’s so simple!</p>
<p>But other times, not so much.</p>
<p>Take a good barbecue sauce or rub, for example. I’ve found many recipes that are delicious and not, technically speaking, difficult to make. But there are usually a <em>lot</em> of ingredients and maybe an extended simmer time (for a sauce), which makes the process tedious and time consuming.</p>
<p>When you have an open Sunday, no plans to speak of, recipes like this can be meditative. But sometimes it’s not a lazy Sunday. Sometimes it’s a busy Tuesday night, and you just need something to season your chicken and/or dip it in pronto.</p>
<p>Hence why this comment from the last time we sold this Kosmos stuff caught my eye.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>May not be as good as making thorough homemade flavorings, but for the ease, they’re great.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That’s from <a href="/@Dorkfish92">@Dorkfish92</a>, who goes on to give a very thorough review of the Kosmos products they’ve tried. Here are the highlights:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Nashville Hot Rub</strong>: “great (could be a bit hotter imo)”</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Honey BBQ</strong>: “only okay, it’s almost too sweet and slightly artificial tasting, like a BBQ chip (my wife really liked it though)”</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Peach Habenero</strong>: “a bit weird, but works surprisingly well”</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Double Pepper Cow Cover</strong>: “I have the regular Cow Cover, but end up adding more pepper, so I assume this one would be similar”; “Decent flavor and bark on ribs, though a bit salty”; “used it more sprinkling it over smashburgers towards the end”</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="/@heavyq">@heavyq</a> chimes in with some love for the other sauce:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the OP X-1 is great. It’s been my go-to for ribs lately. My kids love it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>TL;DR: While not everything is going to be to everyone’s tastes, it is pretty good. And also, convenient.</p>
<p>Then again, we sold 808 6-packs of this stuff last time, so if any newly initiated Kosmos consumers want to chime in with their take, that would be much appreciated.</p>